= Cardiac output =

== Overview ==
'''Cardiac output''' is the volume of blood pumped by a ventricle per unit time, conventionally expressed in litres per minute. It is a central quantity in cardiovascular physiology and hemodynamics.

== Heart rate and stroke volume ==
A common physiological relation is:

<pre>
cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume
</pre>

'''Heart rate''' counts heartbeats per minute. '''Stroke volume''' is the blood volume ejected by a ventricle per beat; under the usual ventricular-volume model, it equals end-diastolic volume minus end-systolic volume. A heart rate of 75 beats per minute and a stroke volume of 70 mL per beat yield 5,250 mL per minute, or 5.25 L/min. This is an arithmetic illustration, not a clinical reference range.

== Physiological determinants ==
Stroke volume reflects ventricular filling, contractility and afterload, among other influences. Heart rate reflects intrinsic rhythm and neural, hormonal and physiological influences. Exercise, posture, illness, medication and cardiovascular function may change both quantities. The relation is a bookkeeping identity for averaged beats, not a complete mechanistic model of the heart.

== Pressure, flow, and arterial pulse ==
[[Related to::Blood pressure|Blood pressure]] and cardiac output measure different quantities: the first is pressure, the second volumetric flow rate. In simplified steady-circulation models, a pressure gradient relates to flow and vascular resistance; real circulation is pulsatile, spatially complex and elastic.

[[Related to::Pulse|Pulse]] examination can estimate beat frequency and rhythm, but it cannot alone determine the volume ejected with each beat. Thus pulse rate by itself does not determine cardiac output.

== Measurement and limitations ==
Cardiac output may be measured or estimated by techniques that rely on different physiological assumptions, such as echocardiographic flow estimation, indicator dilution or invasive hemodynamic methods. Estimates should identify the method, measurement uncertainty and clinical context. A value alone does not establish a patient's diagnosis.

